#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186197941 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c38eccf8-9941-58de-888c-c44130ed48da" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Repeated Integral as a Mixed Integral"}] :question/id #uuid "c8dd9065-9db8-53da-b6a5-cf6cddd3a3e6" :question/math-academy-id "e-11675" :question/problem "Change the order of integration in the integral\n$\\displaystyle \\int_0^1 \\int_0^{2} \\int_0^{\\sqrt[3]{z}} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}z$\nso that it is integrated first with respect to $z$, then over a region $D$ in the $xy$-plane. The integration region $R$ is shown above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4158/Source/Images/dc30042ba8467a4dd3b57e4729cf275e.png)" :question/worked-solution "We wish to find the limits of integration $u_1(x,y)$ and $u_2(x,y)$ such that\n\n$$\n\\int_0^1 \\int_0^{2} \\int_0^{\\sqrt[3]{z}} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}z = \\iint\\limits_D \\left[ \\int_{u_1(x,y)}^{u_2(x,y)} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\right] \\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type III region as follows:\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{(x,y,z) \\:\\: {:} \\:\\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq \\sqrt[3]{z} \\big\\}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}z$ to $\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}A$, we write $R$ as a type I region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $z$-axis and in the direction of increasing $z$, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4158/Source/Images/20ba647fd3e3402dc4ba57a1efe199b3.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the bottom boundary and leaves through the top boundary:\n\n- The bottom boundary of the region, written in the form $z=z(x,y)$, is\n$y = \\sqrt[3]{z}, \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad z = y^3$.\n- The top boundary of the region is $z = 1$.\n\nTherefore, the type I representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{(x,y,z) \\:\\: {:} \\:\\: (x,y) \\in D, \\:\\: y^3\\leq z \\leq 1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4158/Source/Images/54727f66b4a3d3b312c032189ed08a48.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration so that it is integrated first with respect to $z$ and then over $D$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\int_0^1 \\int_0^{2} \\int_0^{\\sqrt[3]{z}} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}z = \\iint\\limits_D \\left[\\int_{y^3}^{1} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\right] \\textrm{d}A\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186197942 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1e87509d-95fe-5804-a94b-d42ada81e4f7" :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration in a Triple Integral"}] :question/id #uuid "83df52ed-139b-5dff-8b32-942aafeff0ba" :question/math-academy-id "e-11676" :question/problem "Change the order of integration in the integral\n$\\displaystyle \\int_0^{1} \\int_0^{1} \\int_0^{1-z^2} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x$.\nso that it is integrated first with respect to $z$, then $y$, and then $x$." :question/worked-solution "We wish to find limits of integration $u_1(x,y)$, $u_2(x,y)$, $v_1(x)$, $v_2(x)$, $a$, and $b$ such that\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{1} \\int_0^{1} \\int_0^{1-z^2} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x = \\int_a^b \\int_{v_1(x)}^{v_2(x)} \\int_{u_1(x,y)}^{u_2(x,y)} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type III region as follows:\n\n$$\nR = \\left\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1-z^2 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region. Since it is a type III region, we first draw the projection onto the $xz$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nE = \\left\\{(x,z) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4158/Source/Images/0898e2ab1dad983d6db2c113b25a7b9b.png)\n\nThen, the region $R$ is shown in the diagram below, where\n\n$$\n0 \\leq y \\leq 1-z^2\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4158/Source/Images/7bf3173bf663a363a02d25d6025c92dc.png)\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x$ to $\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x$, we write $R$ as a type I region and its projection $D$ onto the $xy$-plane as a type I *plane* region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $z$-axis and in the direction of increasing $z$, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4158/Source/Images/54b1cbed41d7992355278fe388a141c6.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the bottom boundary and leaves through the top boundary:\n\n- The top boundary of the region, written in the form $z=z(x,y)$, is\n$y = 1-z^2 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad z = \\sqrt{1- y}$.\n- The bottom boundary of the region is $z=0$.\n\nTherefore, the type I representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: (x,y) \\in D, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq \\sqrt{1-y} \\big\\}\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4158/Source/Images/5a04ed84a421e394756b01cbdd473210.png)\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ as a type I plane region in the $xy$-plane with the top curve given by $y=1$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4158/Source/Images/84fb222f81aaade0074f70c558fa3b30.png)\n\nHence, we have that\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration so that it is integrated first with respect to $z$, then $y$, then $x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{1} \\int_0^{1} \\int_0^{1-z^2} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x = \\int_0^{1} \\int_0^{\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{1}} \\int_{\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{0}}^{\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\sqrt{1-y}}} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186197966 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c1eaf02c-e29b-58dd-aa24-8b0bb70183a0" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Triple Integral by Swapping the Order of Integration"}] :question/id #uuid "15684778-4ac6-5741-b17b-4ba9f8a193e4" :question/math-academy-id "e-12405" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{2} \\int_{z}^{2} e^{x^2} \\, \\mathrm{d}x \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\mathrm{d}y$.\n\n*Hint: Change the order of integration to $\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}y$.*" :question/worked-solution "We cannot integrate the given integral because we cannot find a simple antiderivative of the integrand. So, we change the order of integration.\n\nWe wish to find the limits of integration $u_1(x,y)$, $u_2(x,y)$, $v_1(y)$, $v_2(y)$, $a$, and $b$ such that\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{0}^{2} \\int_{z}^{2} e^{x^2} \\, \\mathrm{d}x \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\mathrm{d}y = \\int_{a}^{b} \\int_{v_1(y)}^{v_2(y)} \\int_{u_1(x,y)}^{u_2(x,y)} e^{x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}z \\: \\textrm{d}x \\: \\textrm{d}y\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nR = \\left\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 2, \\:\\: z \\leq x \\leq 2 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region. Since it is a type II region, we first sketch the projection onto the $yz$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nE = \\left\\{(y,z) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 2 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4158/Source/Images/50867af2658a2fd8436bc8f1c49b01d9.png)\n\nThen, the region $R$ is shown in the diagram below, where\n\n$$\nz \\leq x \\leq 2\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4158/Source/Images/183a99ad9f325c9c75801f18e667d562.png)\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\mathrm{d}x \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\mathrm{d}y$ to $\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}y$, we write $R$ as a type I region and its projection $D$ onto the $xy$-plane as a type II *plane* region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $z$-axis and in the direction of increasing $z$, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4158/Source/Images/0f5cb134455e0cfceef74be513903e4a.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the bottom boundary and leaves through the top boundary:\n\n- The bottom boundary of the region is\n$z=0$.\n- The top boundary of the region, written in the form $z = z(x,y)$, is\n$x = z \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad z = x$.\n\nTherefore, the type I representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR = \\left\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: (x,y) \\in D, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq x \\right\\}\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4158/Source/Images/e4de33214185ed8a7bebf8a9db1b1051.png)\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ as a type II plane region in the $xy$-plane with the left curve given by $x = 0$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4158/Source/Images/8b095ecbfe344bc149ece073098d6a15.png)\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{2}∫_{z}^{2}e^{x^{2}}dxdzdy &= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{2}∫_{0}^{x}e^{x^{2}}dzdxdy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{2}e^{x^{2}}[∫_{0}^{x}dz]dxdy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{2}e^{x^{2}}[z]_{0}^{x}dxdy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{2}xe^{x^{2}}dxdy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}[∫_{0}^{2}xe^{x^{2}}dx]dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}[\\frac{1}{2}e^{x^{2}}]_{0}^{2}dy \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}∫_{0}^{1}(e^{4} - 1)dy \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(e^{4} - 1) \\cdot [y]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(e^{4} - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198887 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c38eccf8-9941-58de-888c-c44130ed48da" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Repeated Integral as a Mixed Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b2cc5cb-2bfb-5a02-9fe1-3d0852626e61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∬_(D)[∫_{1}^{2 - x - z}f(x, y, z)dy]dA" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197558} {:answer/id #uuid "c18c43d1-c821-5a67-bf85-f5b15c1212a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∬_(D)[∫_{0}^{2 - x - z}f(x, y, z)dy]dA" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197559} {:answer/id #uuid "d5055593-8c38-5558-95e6-0db9b3403211" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∬_(D)[∫_{0}^{(2 - x - z)/2}f(x, y, z)dy]dA" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197560} {:answer/id #uuid "dbf46a50-7280-5e3d-95b9-92dfa349c4cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∬_(D)[∫_{0}^{2 - x - 2z}f(x, y, z)dy]dA" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197561} {:answer/id #uuid "71e3c7cb-903c-5b74-b846-0e3629b769a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∬_(D)[∫_{2 - z}^{(2 - x - z)/2}f(x, y, z)dy]dA" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197562}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5055593-8c38-5558-95e6-0db9b3403211" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∬_(D)[∫_{0}^{(2 - x - z)/2}f(x, y, z)dy]dA" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197560} :answer-field/id #uuid "7637f095-c93f-51f0-9159-b237137037e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197557}] :question/id #uuid "28fccb5e-7a4d-52e5-a38d-41ce9d967777" :question/math-academy-id "q-38980" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4158/Source/Images/q-38980.png)\n\nChange the order of integration in the integral\n$∫_{0}^{2}∫_{0}^{(2 - x)/2}∫_{0}^{2 - x - 2y}f(x, y, z)dzdydx$\nso that it is integrated first with respect to $y$, then over a region $D$ in the $xz$-plane. The integration region $R$ is shown above."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198896 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c1eaf02c-e29b-58dd-aa24-8b0bb70183a0" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Triple Integral by Swapping the Order of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0d5e63e-20b0-51be-828c-9acb43bf5635" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e^{2} - 1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197612} {:answer/id #uuid "fe047bf5-e386-5cb6-b074-a4af7209cc5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e - 1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197613} {:answer/id #uuid "037e947e-5b63-53b6-a270-f8fc01b73549" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197614} {:answer/id #uuid "52012d86-6782-596d-bdaf-cff1ea568658" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{2} - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197615} {:answer/id #uuid "6476b2b8-3814-5397-8637-9d2afc0455b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e - 1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197616}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe047bf5-e386-5cb6-b074-a4af7209cc5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e - 1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197613} :answer-field/id #uuid "400e8c38-4fa2-516e-a75c-eec6573ce5a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197611}] :question/id #uuid "bf062656-4e4e-5cbe-a3a9-68966303708b" :question/math-academy-id "q-39154" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4158/Source/Images/q-39154.png)\n\nEvaluate\n$∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{1}∫_{\\sqrt{y}}^{1}e^{z^{3}}dzdydx$.\nThe region of integration is shown above.\n\n*Hint: Change the order of integration to $dydzdx$.*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198897 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c1eaf02c-e29b-58dd-aa24-8b0bb70183a0" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Triple Integral by Swapping the Order of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b66340fc-0448-509f-bdd0-70f63048085f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197618} {:answer/id #uuid "c3e5f6c7-7d42-5233-86ff-47c9bf6a1e3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197619} {:answer/id #uuid "f05e8bff-5afb-53ae-b0e8-eb875051bfd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197620} {:answer/id #uuid "fefc612b-9c21-5a5c-a197-315432ecde3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197621} {:answer/id #uuid "5e4f91c9-d66d-5c27-954f-9c5de8bcede2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197622}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3e5f6c7-7d42-5233-86ff-47c9bf6a1e3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197619} :answer-field/id #uuid "f58ad5aa-d3ac-54dc-b361-02ddd2c3f413" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197617}] :question/id #uuid "b2d988e7-dd3f-5642-ad43-14d09def5c35" :question/math-academy-id "q-39415" :question/problem "$∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{π}}∫_{z^{2}}^{\\sqrt{π}}z\\cos (\\frac{π}{6} + y^{2})dydzdx =$\n\n*Hint: Change the order of integration to $dzdydx$.*"}]}
